<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-21077] use $LST path in lst.sh and sanity-lnet</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-21077</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;    lst.sh invoked the 'lst' binary by bare name, so it only worked when&lt;br/&gt;
    lst was installed in the PATH. When running from a build tree lst is&lt;br/&gt;
    built in lnet/utils/ and is not installed, so sanity-lnet tests 220,&lt;br/&gt;
    228 and 257 failed instead of running (or skipping cleanly).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;    Resolve the lst binary in lst.sh: honour an $LST full pathname if one&lt;br/&gt;
    is given, otherwise look for $LUSTRE/../lnet/utils/lst, and finally&lt;br/&gt;
    fall back to the PATH. All lst invocations now use $LST, and the&lt;br/&gt;
    "cannot find lst" error reports the pathname that was tried.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;    In test-framework.sh add $LUSTRE/../lnet/utils to the PATH alongside&lt;br/&gt;
    the other build-tree utility directories, and silence the stderr of&lt;br/&gt;
    the 'which lst' fallback when lst is not installed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;    In sanity-lnet.sh add check_lst_tools() so tests that need lst.sh and&lt;br/&gt;
    lst skip with a clear message when those tools are unavailable, rather&lt;br/&gt;
    than failing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;    Also replace the load_modules() call in cleanup_testsuite() with a new&lt;br/&gt;
    restore_lnet() helper that reloads only the LNet modules that&lt;br/&gt;
    cleanup_lnet() unloaded. This suite never needs the server stack, and&lt;br/&gt;
    load_modules() additionally pulls in the OSD modules, which can fail&lt;br/&gt;
    on nodes where their dependencies are not loadable - e.g. osd_zfs&lt;br/&gt;
    needs ZFS modules that are not installed for the running kernel.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
